  "type" : "multiple-choice",
  "id" : 264,
  "title" : "Consequences of the Observer pattern",
  "text" : "A stock ticker publishes price updates to many independent dashboard widgets. The ticker keeps a list of registered listeners and calls them in a loop:\n\n```java\npublic void publish(Price price) {\n    for (PriceListener listener : listeners) {\n        listener.onPrice(price);\n    }\n}\n```\n\nWhich statements about this design are correct?",
  "hint" : "Two claims follow from the loop as written, not from the pattern in the abstract.",
  "points" : 2.0,
  "scoringType" : "ALL_OR_NOTHING",
  "randomizeOrder" : false,
  "invalid" : false,
  "answerOptions" : [ {
    "id" : 810,
    "text" : "The ticker needs no compile-time knowledge of the concrete widget classes.",
    "explanation" : "Listeners are known only through the `PriceListener` interface.",
    "isCorrect" : true,
    "invalid" : false
  }, {
    "id" : 811,
    "text" : "A widget that throws inside `onPrice` prevents the remaining widgets from being notified.",
    "explanation" : "The loop has no isolation, so the exception propagates out of `publish`.",
    "isCorrect" : true,
    "invalid" : false
  }, {
    "id" : 812,
    "text" : "Widgets cannot be registered or removed once the ticker has been constructed.",
    "explanation" : "Registration is a runtime operation; that is the point of the pattern.",
    "isCorrect" : false,
    "invalid" : false
  }, {
    "id" : 813,
    "text" : "The notification order is guaranteed by the pattern to match registration order.",
    "explanation" : "The pattern makes no such guarantee; here it is an accident of the list implementation.",
    "isCorrect" : false,
    "invalid" : false
  } ],
  "singleChoice" : false,
  "exerciseId" : 264
